For optimistic updates, you separate the optimistic UI state from the actual server state. Here's a useReducer implementation:

```javascript
const initialState = {
  data: null,
  status: 'idle', // 'idle' | 'pending' | 'success' | 'error'
  previousData: null,
  error: null
};

function reducer(state, action) {
  switch (action.type) {
    case 'OPTIMISTIC_UPDATE':
      return {
        ...state,
        previousData: state.data,
        data: action.payload,
        status: 'pending'
      };
    case 'SUCCESS':
      return {
        ...state,
        status: 'success',
        previousData: null
      };
    case 'ERROR':
      return {
        ...state,
        status: 'error',
        data: state.previousData, // rollback
        previousData: null,
        error: action.error
      };
    default:
      return state;
  }
}

// Usage in component:
const [state, dispatch] = useReducer(reducer, initialState);

const handleUpdate = async (newData) => {
  dispatch({ type: 'OPTIMISTIC_UPDATE', payload: newData });
  try {
    await api.update(newData);
    dispatch({ type: 'SUCCESS' });
  } catch (error) {
    dispatch({ type: 'ERROR', error });
  }
};
```

The useState approach uses useEffect for the server call:

```javascript
const [data, setData] = useState(null);
const [isPending, setIsPending] = useState(false);
const [error, setError] = useState(null);

const handleUpdate = async (newData) => {
  const previousData = data;
  setData(newData); // optimistic update
  setIsPending(true);
  try {
    await api.update(newData);
  } catch (error) {
    setData(previousData); // rollback
    setError(error);
  } finally {
    setIsPending(false);
  }
};
```

For race conditions with multiple rapid updates, use AbortController to cancel in-flight requests:

```javascript
const abortRef = useRef(null);

const handleUpdate = async (newData) => {
  // Cancel any in-flight request
  abortRef.current?.abort();

  const controller = new AbortController();
  abortRef.current = controller;

  setData(newData); // optimistic
  try {
    await api.update(newData, { signal: controller.signal });
  } catch (error) {
    if (error.name !== 'AbortError') {
      setData(previousData);
    }
  }
};
```

Alternatively, use version/sequence tracking to ignore stale responses:

```javascript
const versionRef = useRef(0);

const handleUpdate = async (newData) => {
  const currentVersion = ++versionRef.current;
  setData(newData);
  try {
    await api.update(newData);
    if (currentVersion !== versionRef.current) return; // stale response
    // process success
  } catch (error) {
    if (currentVersion !== versionRef.current) return;
    setData(previousData); // rollback
  }
};
```

Key strategies: 1) Store previous state before optimistic update, 2) Use AbortController to cancel stale requests, 3) Track request version to ignore outdated responses, 4) Consider a queue for sequential processing of dependent updates.

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to design high-frequency data handling in React with Redux and Context, focusing on minimizing re-renders during bursts while supporting multiple views. It probes knowledge of selectors, memoization, and rendering strategies in real-world data load scenarios.

## Key Concepts

- Memoized selectors (reselect) for derived data
- React.memo for pure chart components
- useTransition to defer updates during bursts
- Virtualization/windowing to handle large bucket lists
- Redux data flow with high-rate streams and separate UI state in Context

## Code Example

```javascript
// Memoized selector sketch (conceptual)
import { createSelector } from '@reduxjs/toolkit';

const selectEvents = (state) => state.metrics.events; // raw events stream

const bucketizeBySecond = createSelector(
  [selectEvents],
  (events) => {
    const buckets = [];
    let current = [];
    let currentSecond = null;
    for (const e of events) {
      const t = Math.floor(e.ts / 1000) * 1000;
      if (currentSecond !== t) {
        if (current.length) buckets.push(current);
        current = [e];
        currentSecond = t;
      } else {
        current.push(e);
      }
    }
    if (current.length) buckets.push(current);
    return buckets;
  }
);
```

